  <data name="en_pano45_description"><![CDATA[Welcome to Military Cemetery Zungeru, Niger State. Here lays the remains of colonial masters who died in their stride to colonize Nigeria. Zungeru is the birth place of the Nigerian nation. It was in this tiny little town that the amalgamation of the Northern and Southern protectorates was sealed by the British colonialists led by the Late Sir Lord Fredrick Lugard in 1914. The Name “Nigeria” was given to the new Nation by Lady Flora, and it is made out of these two words “Niger and Area” which mean the “Region of River Niger”.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano46_description"><![CDATA[<div>To many Nigerians, the ancient town, noted as the abode of railway workers, had played significant roles in the political developments of the nation.</div><div>Apart from its colonial history, &nbsp;Zungeru has other places of historical interest which includes the family &nbsp;houses &nbsp;of &nbsp;the first Nigerian indigenous president, the late Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe, and that of Chief Chukwuemeka Odumegwu Ojukwu, the Ikemba of Nnewi, who were both born in Zungeru, as their parents worked as clerks to colonial masters.</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano47_description"><![CDATA[Also Laid at this Cemetery is the founder of the Boy Scout Movement, Sir Henry James Speed.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano48_description"><![CDATA[At this part section of the cemetery lays the remains of Nigerian (Blacks) who died during the colonial administration in Zungeru.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano49_description"><![CDATA[Zungeru was established by the Lord Fredrick Lugard in 1900 as military and administrative settlement, with the aim of protecting the colonized area of the Country from the then Northern invaders. The first organized military “The West Africa Frontier force” which latter emerged into the present day Nigerian Army, was set up in Zungaru.]]></data>


